-- title:
--   Proposition 4.2 — sharp degree–distance criterion: $2D<d_{\mathrm{rel}}$ iff $\pi$ preserves $\tau(X^*Y)$ on $\mathcal F^A_D$
-- statement:
--   Let $H_A=\mathbb C^{N_A}$ and $H_B=\mathbb C^{N_B}$ be finite-dimensional Hilbert spaces, let $a_1,\dots,a_s$ be operators on $H_A$ and $b_1,\dots,b_s$ operators on $H_B$ (no further assumption on them), and let $\mathcal A=C^*(I_A,a_1,\dots,a_s)$ be the unital $*$-algebra they generate. Let $\pi:\mathcal A\to B(H_B)$ be a unital $*$-homomorphism with $\pi(a_i)=b_i$. Write $\tau_L(X)=\operatorname{Tr}X/N_L$ for the normalized trace, $\|X\|_{2,L}=\sqrt{\tau_L(X^*X)}$, $\mathcal F^A_t$ for the span of the identity and of all marked words $a_{i_1}^{\epsilon_1}\cdots a_{i_\ell}^{\epsilon_\ell}$ with $\ell\le t$ and $\epsilon_j\in\{1,*\}$, and $d_{\mathrm{rel}}\in\mathbb N_0\cup\{+\infty\}$ for the relative distance (Definition 4.1).
--
--   Then for every integer $D\ge0$:
--
--   1. the condition
--
--   $$2D<d_{\mathrm{rel}}$$
--
--   is equivalent to
--
--   $$\tau_B(\pi(X)^*\pi(Y))=\tau_A(X^*Y)\qquad(X,Y\in\mathcal F^A_D);$$
--
--   2. in particular, if $2D<d_{\mathrm{rel}}$, then $\pi$ is an isometry from $\mathcal F^A_D$ onto $\mathcal F^B_D=\pi(\mathcal F^A_D)$: $\|\pi(X)\|_{2,B}=\|X\|_{2,A}$ for every $X\in\mathcal F^A_D$, and $\pi$ is injective on $\mathcal F^A_D$.
--
--   The criterion says exactly how far a homomorphism, which always transports polynomials, also transports the normalized operator states: every degree-$D$ polynomial state of the source is carried to the corresponding target state with its normalization intact precisely when $2D$ is below the relative distance. It is the algebraic basis of homomorphic polynomial transduction by relative decoding.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - generalization: Hilbert spaces H_A, H_B with N_L = dim H_L ≥ 1 (τ_L divides by N_L) → index types nA, nB may be empty, where Lean's x/0 = 0 gives τ_L ≡ 0; reason: covers only extra degenerate instances where the source's τ_L is undefined; on nonempty index types it is the source's statement verbatim
--   - encoding: A = C∗(I_A, a₁,…,a_s) ⊆ B(H_A), π : A ↠ B unital ∗-homomorphism with π(aᵢ) = bᵢ → A = StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ (Set.range a) in Matrix nA nA ℂ; π : A →⋆ₐ[ℂ] Matrix nB nB ℂ with π (gen a i) = b i; reason: in finite dimension the generated ∗-subalgebra is norm closed (App. E, p.85); the range of π is the ∗-subalgebra generated by the bᵢ, i.e. B, so 'onto B' is automatic
--   - encoding: marked words a_{i₁}^{ϵ₁}⋯a_{i_ℓ}^{ϵ_ℓ}, ϵⱼ ∈ {1, ∗}, empty word = I_A; 𝓕ᴬ_t their span for ℓ ≤ t → words are List (Fin s × Bool) with true = adjoint, evaluated by wordEval; 𝓕ᴬ_t = markedSpace a t, a Submodule ℂ of A; reason: Lean convention; same spaces
--   - encoding: d_rel ∈ ℕ₀ ∪ {+∞}, empty minimum +∞ → relDist a π : ℕ∞ as sInf of the image in ℕ∞, sInf ∅ = ⊤; reason: Lean convention; same values
--   - encoding: π is an isometry from 𝓕ᴬ_D onto 𝓕ᴮ_D → no separate surjectivity clause; 𝓕ᴮ_D is the image π(𝓕ᴬ_D); reason: (43) defines 𝓕ᴮ_D = π(𝓕ᴬ_D), so 'onto' holds by definition
--   - reading: 'In particular π is an isometry from 𝓕ᴬ_D onto 𝓕ᴮ_D' → the clause is stated under 2D < d_rel as norm preservation and injectivity on 𝓕ᴬ_D; reason: the sentence after the proof ('In particular, π is injective on 𝓕ᴬ_D') and App. A.4 ((358) and 'therefore π is injective on V', applied to V = 𝓕ᴬ_D) give these two forms; this is the stronger of the readings
-- source:
--   Shang, Stilck França, From Simple Sources to Quantum Advantage: Homomorphic Polynomial Transduction via Relative Decoding, arXiv:2609.35101 (held version, sha256 d2be7859…), Proposition 4.2, eqs. (47)–(48), p.18 (pdf_page 18), with the sentence after its proof (p.18) and App. A.4 (p.68)

import Mathlib
import Definitions.Def_LIDT_MIPStarRE_Quantum_FiniteMatrix_NormalizedTrace
import Definitions.Def_RelDecoding_markedSpace
import Definitions.Def_RelDecoding_norm2
import Definitions.Def_RelDecoding_relDist

set_option autoImplicit false

namespace RelDecoding

open LIDT.MIPStarRE.Quantum

/-- **Proposition 4.2 (Sharp degree–distance criterion).** For an integer `D ≥ 0`, the condition
`2D < d_rel` (47) is equivalent to `τ_B(π(X)∗π(Y)) = τ_A(X∗Y)` for all `X, Y ∈ 𝓕ᴬ_D` (48). In
particular, when `2D < d_rel`, `π` is an isometry from `𝓕ᴬ_D` onto `𝓕ᴮ_D = π(𝓕ᴬ_D)`:
`∥π(X)∥_{2,B} = ∥X∥_{2,A}` for `X ∈ 𝓕ᴬ_D`, and `π` is injective on `𝓕ᴬ_D`. Shang, Stilck França,
*From Simple Sources to Quantum Advantage: Homomorphic Polynomial Transduction via Relative
Decoding*, arXiv:2609.35101 (held version, sha256 d2be7859…), Proposition 4.2, p.18 (and the
sentence after its proof: "In particular, π is injective on 𝓕ᴬ_D").

**Formalization Note.** `A = C∗(I_A, a₁, …, a_s)` is `StarAlgebra.adjoin ℂ (Set.range a)` inside
`Matrix nA nA ℂ` (in finite dimension the generated ∗-subalgebra is the generated C∗-algebra);
`π` is any unital ∗-homomorphism from `A` into `Matrix nB nB ℂ` with `π(aᵢ) = bᵢ`, whose range is
`B = C∗(I_B, b)`; `τ_L = Tr / N_L` (`normalizedTrace`), `∥X∥_{2,L} = √(τ_L(X∗X))` (`norm2`);
`𝓕ᴬ_D` is the span of marked words of length at most `D` (`markedSpace`); `d_rel ∈ ℕ∞` with `+∞`
for the empty minimum (`relDist`). "Onto `𝓕ᴮ_D`" holds by the definition `𝓕ᴮ_D = π(𝓕ᴬ_D)`. The
index types may be empty (then `τ ≡ 0` by Lean's `x / 0 = 0`). -/
theorem proposition_4_2 {nA nB : Type*} [Fintype nA] [DecidableEq nA] [Fintype nB]
    [DecidableEq nB] {s : ℕ} (a : Fin s → Matrix nA nA ℂ) (b : Fin s → Matrix nB nB ℂ)
    (π : genAlg a →⋆ₐ[ℂ] Matrix nB nB ℂ) (hπ : ∀ i, π (gen a i) = b i) (D : ℕ) :
    ((((2 * D : ℕ) : ℕ∞) < relDist a π ↔
        ∀ X ∈ markedSpace a D, ∀ Y ∈ markedSpace a D,
          normalizedTrace (star (π X) * π Y) =
            normalizedTrace (star (X : Matrix nA nA ℂ) * (Y : Matrix nA nA ℂ))) ∧
      (((2 * D : ℕ) : ℕ∞) < relDist a π →
        (∀ X ∈ markedSpace a D, norm2 (π X) = norm2 (X : Matrix nA nA ℂ)) ∧
          Set.InjOn π (markedSpace a D : Set (genAlg a)))) := by sorry

end RelDecoding
